  <h2>4 Exposure & Color Metrics Every Cinematographer Must Master</h2>
  <p><b>1. Nits Luminance Rating vs. Direct Sun:</b> Standard 500 Nits monitors perform admirably in controlled indoor studio shadows, but instantly wash out under direct outdoor midday sunlight without physical sunhood visors. Ultra-bright displays like the 2800 Nits Desview R6 remain crystal-clear outdoors.<br>
  <b>2. False Color IRE Chart for S-Log3 / C-Log2:</b> To obtain reliable skin tones without guessing exposure, the professional False Color mapping tool renders wheatish Indian skin tones at roughly 40-45 IRE (Green zone) and fairer skin profiles around 60-65 IRE (Pink zone). Overexposed highlight blowouts visually trigger solid Red warnings (100+ IRE).<br>
  <b>3. Real-Time RGB Waveform Scope:</b> Unlike a basic brightness histogram, a professional Waveform RGB Parade displays precise left-to-right positional data, informing you exactly which corner of your frame architecture suffers from blown exposure or tint color skew.<br>
  <b>4. Custom 3D LUT Compatibility (.CUBE):</b> Onboard SD card slots enable cinematographers to ingest standard 33x33 or 65x65 `.CUBE` LUT tables directly into the monitoring signal—providing directors and clients with real-time graded visual previews while continuing to capture flat Log files.</p>

